          results. The headings below say what was actually exercised. The procedure was
          designed from:
            - the PIM-SM protocol itself (RFC 4601). The mechanisms a PIM-SM verdict
              must show are the PIM neighbour adjacency, the group-to-RP mapping, the
              Register path from the first-hop DR to the RP, the RP's Join toward the
              source, Register-Stop, the tree carrying data, and prune on leave;
            - the DUT's own CLI surface, read read-only with tools/qmark.py: global
              `ip multicast-routing [border|ssm-only-hw|vrf]`, `ip pim rp-address|ssm|
              bsr-candidate|rp-candidate|spt-threshold|register-*|vrf`; interface
              `ip pim sparse-mode|dense-mode|dr-priority|hello-interval|neighbor-filter|
              state-refresh`; `show ip pim sparse-mode ...`, `show ip mroute [sparse|
              dense|static|count|summary|vrf]`;
            - the rule that a multicast verdict may NOT rest on `show ip mroute`. Every
              claim below is a counted far-side result on a host NIC, or a packet decode
              from the DUT's own tcpdump on the routed-only transit VLAN. The `show`
              reads are corroboration only.
          There is no multicast Feature Overview Guide on this share, and ck.db's
          `cli_commands` corpus holds no x960 pages, so no document is quoted for this
          platform either. 8 steps as executed, plus a baseline.
