VERDICT: PASS -- PIM-SM routes IPv4 multicast between two routers on this build. The
proof is counted traffic at a far-side host NIC with an in-leg control group that must
not arrive, plus the Register state machine decoded frame by frame on the transit wire;
the table reads only corroborate.
      baseline, multicast routing OFF ..... joined receiver, 0 of 10 group frames
                                            forwarded; routed unicast control 5/5
      PIM-SM forwarding ................... 5 of 5 for the joined group in steady
                                            state, egress MAC 88:9d:98:df:26:02
                                            (= swi_a's, so both routers forwarded it)
      never-joined control group .......... 0 of 5 in the SAME leg, every leg
      Register -> RP Join -> data ......... Register at 03:43:39.659544, RP Join/Prune
                                            0.6 ms later, native data 340 ms after that
      Register-Stop ....................... 03:45:03.355179, once the SPT is up; null
                                            Registers (28 B) continue as keepalives
      prune on leave ...................... the LEFT group 0 of 5 while a still-joined
                                            group is 5 of 5 in the same leg
      the RP is load-bearing .............. A/B on ONE group: RP mapping removed 0 of
                                            10, RP mapping restored 8 of 10
      router-side IGMP .................... `ip pim sparse-mode` alone does NOT enable
                                            it; see STEP 2 (finding F-13)
      reboot persistence .................. a group first used AFTER the reload forwards
                                            8 of 10; control 0 of 10
      removal ............................. `no ip pim sparse-mode` -> 0 of 10 for a
                                            group that was forwarding 8/10 minutes before
CAVEAT:  Two PIM-SM limbs this bench cannot reach, named rather than silently omitted.
         ASSERT needs two PIM routers on ONE shared segment and the only multi-router
         segment here is a /30. BSR / candidate-RP election needs a third PIM router;
         `ip pim bsr-candidate` and `ip pim rp-candidate` exist on the DUT but only two
         units on this bench hold the PIM licence, so a STATIC RP was used throughout --
         which does exercise the Register path in full. swi_a was configured but never
         reloaded or power-cycled, so reboot persistence is proven for the DUT only.

