STEP 1 -- PIM-SM brought up on both routers: service gate, adjacency, group-to-RP mapping  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: AW+ gates the routing daemons twice -- once on a licensed `service`, once on
  `ip multicast-routing`. Both gates were captured rather than worked around. The
  partner question was settled the same way, before any measurement: swi_b holds
  `L3-MC-ROUTE` but no PIM licence bit, and its `service ?` list offers `pdm`, `pim` and
  `pim6` identically to the other two units, so a read-only surface probe could never
  have settled it. Sending the command does:

x960-28YSQ(config)#service pim
% Service is not currently licensed on this platform
x960-28YSQ(config)#service pdm
% Service is not currently licensed on this platform
                                      <-- swi_b cannot be a PIM router. Nothing was
                                          applied to it: its running-config before and
                                          after holds only `service password-encryption`
                                          and `service ssh`

swi_d(config)#service pim                             accepted 16:32:59
swi_a(config)#service pim                             accepted 16:33:08
swi_d#show process | include pim
 4353 pimd                5   0.0   0.1    20 sleep   <-- the daemon IS running

  The second gate, captured by deliberately configuring PIM before enabling multicast
  routing:

awplus(config-if)#ip pim sparse-mode
% IP Multicast Routing not activated

  `ip multicast-routing` was then sent and `ip pim sparse-mode` accepted. (Note that
  `show ip multicast-routing` is NOT a command on this build: `% Invalid input
  detected`.) The adjacency and the mapping, across a real wire:

awplus#show ip pim sparse-mode neighbor
Neighbor          Interface        Uptime/Expires            Ver   DR
10.0.61.2         vlan61           00:00:57/00:01:18         v2    1 / DR
awplus#show ip pim sparse-mode rp mapping
PIM Group-to-RP Mappings
Group(s): 224.0.0.0/4, Static
    RP: 10.0.61.2

  => STEP 1 PASS: a PIM v2 adjacency is established over the transit VLAN between two
     physically separate routers, and both ends resolve 224.0.0.0/4 to the RP at
     10.0.61.2. The licence gate is exact rather than inferred -- swi_b's refusal is a
     one-line verdict, and it is why swi_a is the partner.

